# Unversioned and deliberate: speedtest-cli ships /usr/bin/speedtest, the same
# path Ookla's engine installs to, so no version of the two can coexist.
Conflicts: speedtest-cli
Description: scheduled internet speed tests with a complete local history
 Bandwidth Logger measures your internet connection at an interval you choose
 and keeps a permanent, timestamped record of every test on this computer. It
 is built for diagnosing slowdowns that come and go: instead of a single
 reading taken when you happen to notice a problem, you get a history you can
 look through and export.
 .
 Every attempted test is recorded, including ones that failed, timed out or
 were skipped, so a gap in the history always has a stated reason.
 .
 Results are stored in SQLite in your home directory and can be exported to
 CSV in full or for a date range. Scheduling uses a systemd user timer, so
 tests continue after the window is closed and resume after you log back in.
 .
 Nothing leaves the computer except the speed test itself. There is no
 account, no cloud service and no analytics, and the application never needs
 administrator rights to run.
 .
 Measurements are taken by the official Ookla Speedtest CLI, the same engine
 speedtest.net uses. Its licence does not permit redistribution, so it is not
 included here; on first launch the application explains that it is Ookla's
 software and guides you through installing it.
 .
 This package conflicts with speedtest-cli, which claims the same
 /usr/bin/speedtest filename and would prevent Ookla's engine from being
 installed. An open-source fallback was offered in version 1.0.0 and removed
 in 1.1.0 because it under-reported badly on fast connections.
